    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: reject with 400 when the owner's email domain is on a disposable-domain blocklist (mailinator.com, tempmail.com, guerrillamail.com).\n\nI kept structural impact minimal by mirroring the existing validation pattern used for postcode and registration date rather than touching the large controller class:\n\n1. **New file** `service/DisposableEmailDomain.java` \u2014 a small, cohesive validator with a single static `validate` method (no surrounding class complexity) that throws `IllegalArgumentException` for a blocklisted domain. The existing `ExceptionControllerAdvice.handleIllegalArgumentException` already maps that to 400.\n\n2. **One-line change** in `OwnerMapper.toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` \u2014 added a `@Mapping` expression wiring the email through the new validator, exactly like the sibling `PostcodeValidator`/`RegistrationDateValidator` mappings.\n\nThis avoids editing `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (a method inside a large, high-WMC class), so the only changed function is the mapper's abstract method (CC 1) plus a brand-new low-complexity unit. Two files touched total.\n\n`AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsDisposableDomain` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
